SEMANTIC KERNEL: COMPRESSED LEXICON + DUALITY FRAMEWORK
=====================================================================
004.6 software systems framework

SECTION 1 — RDG PRIMITIVE GRAMMAR (TRIADIC OPERATOR FORM)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
RDG expresses all relational geometry using a 3‑slot operator:

    noun_qualifier^dynamic

Where:
    noun      = base entity / structural node
    qualifier = adjective / property / restriction (underscore)
    dynamic   = verb / transformation / mode (caret)

This triad is the operational collapse of the RDG 4‑seed (point,
counterpoint, mirrorpoint, mirror‑counterpoint). The seed is 4‑fold,
but the operator grammar is 3‑fold.

RDG triad = Structure – Interaction – Dynamics.


SECTION 2 — MFE PRIMITIVE GRAMMAR (BIPOLAR POLYNOMIAL FORM)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
MFE expresses all evaluative flux using a bipolar polynomial:

    (X⁺ , X⁻)

Where:
    X⁺ = positive pole of concept X
    X⁻ = negative pole of concept X

Flux polynomial:
    Φ(X) = X⁺ – X⁻

Evaluation:
    E(X) = f(Φ(X))

Q‑slice moderation:
    Q(X) = moderation(E(X))

MFE primitive = bipolar antonym pair + flux + evaluation + Q‑slice.


SECTION 3 — RDG/MFE DUALITY RULE (SEMANTIC FILTER)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
A concept is a valid primitive ONLY IF:

    1. It has an objective antonym.
    2. The antonym pair forms a stable bipolar flux.
    3. The flux can be evaluated.
    4. The evaluation can be Q‑moderated.

If ANY of these fail:
    → concept is NOT a valid RDG primitive
    → concept is NOT a valid MFE primitive
    → concept is NOT part of the syntactic keyword set.

This is the RDG–MFE semantic filter.


SECTION 4 — COMPRESSED ENGLISH SOURCES (CANDIDATE POOL)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Three systems provide compressed English concept sets:

    A. NSM (Natural Semantic Metalanguage)
       ~65 semantic primes (conceptual atoms)

    B. WordNet Hypernym Top‑Nodes
       ~40–60 primitive noun categories (structural atoms)

    C. Basic English (850 words)
       surface‑level but broad coverage

Merged candidate pool size:
    ~900–950 concepts.


SECTION 5 — APPLYING THE MFE ANTONYM FILTER
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Each candidate concept is tested:

    Does it have a clear opposite?
    Is the opposite objective?
    Does the pair form a stable polarity?
    Can the flux be evaluated?
    Can the evaluation be Q‑moderated?

If YES → concept survives.
If NO  → concept is removed.

Typical survival counts:
    NSM:       ~20–25 survive
    WordNet:   ~15–20 survive
    Basic Eng: ~100–150 survive

After deduplication:
    ~120–180 true bipolar primitives remain.


SECTION 6 — RESULT: TRUE RDG/MFE SYNTACTIC KEYWORD SET
---------------------------------------------------------------------
The surviving concepts become the actual RDG/MFE primitives:

RDG form:
    noun_qualifier^dynamic

MFE form:
    (noun⁺_qualifier^dynamic) – (noun⁻_qualifier^dynamic)

Q‑slice:
    Q(noun) = moderation(f(noun⁺ – noun⁻))

This set is analogous to:
    - assembly reserved keywords
    - compiler primitive tokens
    - machine‑level semantic operators

This is the true syntactic kernel.


